WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We covered
using Service-Level Indicators

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.140
to measure the reliability
of our services

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.120
and using Service
Level Objectives to set

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.890
reliability targets
in our first episode.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:10.890
But what happens when
that service has a problem

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.780
and we start burning
error budget?

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.370
How can we configure
alerting to let

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.800
us know that our users
are unhappy while managing

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.390
our operational load?

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.940
Today, I'm going to show
you how to calculate and use

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.500
error budget burn
rate thresholds

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.470
as an optimum approach
to your alerting set up.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.170
Welcome to "Engineering for
Reliability" with Google Cloud.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:35.060
To help us, let's review
chapter 5 of the "SRE Workbook"

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.040
called "Alerting on SLOs."

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.870
If you haven't read
it before, no worries.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.210
We'll link to it in the
episode notes below.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.490
The main thing we need to
take away from that chapter

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.460
is that the best way to have
sufficiently sensitive alerts,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.100
while at the same time
reducing false positives,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.190
is to use multi-window,
multi burn-rate alerts.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:53.960
But first, what's a burn rate?

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.610
Error budget burn
rate is a measure

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.010
of how fast,
relative to your SLO,

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.290
your service consumes
error budget.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.740
For example, a service that
has exactly zero error budget

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.110
left at the end of the
SLO evaluation period

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.970
has a burn rate of 1.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.190
A service that consumes
all of its error budget

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.650
halfway through the
SLO evaluation period

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.440
has a burn rate of 2 and so on.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.590
We calculate our burn rate by
looking at three main inputs--

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.810
the SLO and its
evaluation period,

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.120
the amount of error
budget consumed or burned,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.580
and the window over
which that burn happens.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.610
That's our alerting window.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.352
Let's walk through
a specific example.

00:01:30.352 --> 00:01:32.060
Let's say our service
has an availability

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.940
SLO of 95% over a
rolling 28-day window.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.940
We want to know if we're
having a problem that's

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.410
impacting end users.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.510
A great place to
start is to create

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.630
an alert that lets us know that
we've consumed 2% of our error

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.250
budget in the last hour.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:48.260
We use this equation to
calculate the burn rate

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.250
threshold for our
alert, which is 13.44.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.780
We also want to
know if we're seeing

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.560
a slower burn rate, too, 5%
of error budget in six hours.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.060
This time, our burn
rate threshold is 5.6.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.980
We want our alert
to notify us only

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.500
when we're still actively
burning error budget to reduce

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.210
the number of false positives.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.030
To do this, we'll create both
of our alerts with two windows--

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.520
a long window and
a short window.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.760
A good starting place is to
make the long window 12 times

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.320
as long as the short window.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.770
By using multi window alerts,
we keep our detection time low

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:24.170
and minimize our reset
time so that the alert

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:27.170
doesn't keep firing once the
incident has been mitigated.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:28.910
OK, that was a lot.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.620
The main thing to
remember at this point

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.410
is that we can use error budget
burn rate alerts to detect

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.530
issues with our service long
before our SLO is actually

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:37.340
threatened.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.320
Now, let's have a look
at how to set them up

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.210
in Cloud Operations.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.710
Start at the Dashboard page.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.518
Select Monitoring from
the Products menu,

00:02:45.518 --> 00:02:46.435
and click on Services.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.730
Here's a service with an
availability SLO defined

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.380
but no alerts configured.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:58.710
The SLO is evaluated over
a rolling 28-day window.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.830
In order to create an error
budget burn alert for the SLO,

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.060
you need to specify two things.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.000
The first one is the
look-back duration

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.500
or the window over
which the error

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.450
budget burn rate is evaluated.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.570
The second entry is
the burn-rate threshold

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.070
or the value of
the burn rate that

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.620
must be true over the look-back
back window for the alert

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.850
to fire.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.010
To configure a fast error
budget burn rate alert

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.840
that will fire if
2% of error budget

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.290
is burned over an hour, set
the look-back duration to 60

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.920
minutes and the burn
rate threshold to 13.44.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.020
For a slow burn alert that
will fire if 5% of error budget

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.600
is burned over six hours,
set the look-back duration

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.050
to 360 minutes and
the burn rate to 6.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:46.410
Name your loading policy,
specify your notification

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.668
settings for the alert,
provide any documentation

00:03:49.668 --> 00:03:51.210
or additional
information that should

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:56.310
be delivered with the alert,
and save the alert policy.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.950
Finally, confirm that the
alert has been created.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.460
Now, if the availability
of the service degrades,

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:05.512
the alert will fire, and an
incident will be created.

00:04:05.512 --> 00:04:07.470
We've configured an alert
based on error budget

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.110
burn rate threshold, but
what happens when it fires?

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.240
Let's have a look.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.240
Let's see what happens
when an error budget burn

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.310
rate threshold alert fires.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.260
From the Services
Overview page, we

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.410
can see that we have a service
with an SOL alert firing.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:24.890
We can show just those
services with firing alerts.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.690
We can go to Service
Details to confirm

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:29.240
that we have an unhealthy
SLO with a firing alert.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.190
We can expand the SLO
to confirm that we're

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.250
seeing a degradation in our
service level indicator.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.440
On the Error Budget
tab, we can confirm

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:45.150
that we're starting to see
additional error budget burn.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.180
Further down, we can see
the logs being generated

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.950
by the logs for our service.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:53.340
In this case, we're seeing an
increased number of 500 server

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.890
error logs confirming that
our service is currently

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.340
having an issue.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:59.640
Thanks for joining me today.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:00.800
We covered a lot.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.080
You learned about why
alerting on error budget

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.940
burn is a great way
to balance keeping

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.700
users happy against
operational overload.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.950
We then figured out how to
calculate error budget burn

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.840
rates and so how to
use them to set up

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.750
alerting for your services
in Cloud Operations.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:16.033
Thanks so much for watching.

00:05:16.033 --> 00:05:18.200
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.140
on more "Engineering for
Reliability" with Google Cloud.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:22.340
See you soon.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.390
